  dishSprite: {fileID: 1208040086343421634, guid: dbe5bac0b22c963458f1f4116ce19a1c, type: 3}
  hDialogLines:
  - text: Hello in this level the dish we will cook is Dinuguan from Nueva Ejica.
  - text: but first a lets have a short history about Dinuguan
  - text: 'According to Gilbuena, Y. (2021, November 2) Dinuguan (Filipino pork blood
      stew). Serious Eats.

      from https://www.seriouseats.com/dinuguan-filipino-pork-blood-stew-5196555'
  - text: "Dinuguan (\u201Cto be stewed with blood\u201D) is a long-standing Filipino
      stew of pork (often offal) simmered in pig\u2019s blood, vinegar, garlic, and
      chiles."
  - text: 'Its precise origin is unclear, is likely a mix of precolonial practices
      of using whole animals and later cross-cultural influences. '
  - text: But it is well attested in regional variants across the Philippines and
      is traditionally served with puto or rice at fiestas
  - text: Now Lets start cookig.
